cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-87801/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6445834c-7788-5427-a72c-30b06cc8e6ab"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-87801"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-52865"] :question/worked-solution "Let's consider $△CDE.$ We can see that $\\overset{―}{CD}$ is the height corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{DE}$ and that $DE=6\\text{ cm}.$ \n\nSince the area of $△CDE$ is $18{\\text{ cm}}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 18 & =\\frac{6\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\ 3CD & =18 \\\\ CD & =6\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that $\\overset{―}{CD}$ is also the height of $△ABC$ corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945897/assets/87c774cbdce6c4858605e2b8c6186abc6dde73b2b636966b4049ded4f82af684.png)\n\nTherefore, we find the area of $△ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{AB\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 6}{2} \\\\  & =12{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-87910/selection/518537d4c4e164509f4b5289954f6c96b06c713171b31f364abf9c228a6f4154" :answer/id #uuid "c3e51a41-4c4b-5a61-8974-45791a1e48e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36\\,{cm}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-87910/selection/d3cb515841e2411ba12335647dea1373973831398f722271d9be3b1d188bb240" :answer/id #uuid "c2e312d6-48e3-5a41-b6f3-b42015d91870" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "35\\,{cm}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-87910/selection/7893dbe51c638c12da8ed605f53f97ce6d6c4e89a09d73dfae6fcbd01796bb8e" :answer/id #uuid "a39e8353-ca43-58a3-8e15-e04